{
  "id": "ports-producer-consumer-csg",
  "title": "Async port in a game: producer / consumer",
  "chapter": "Use case 5 — Synchronous vs asynchronous ports",
  "chapterOrder": 5,
  "formalism": "csg",
  "tool": "PRISM-games 3.x",
  "summary": "Two players exchange messages through an asynchronous port. In a CSG both move at the same time, so the generated channel is written over joint actions.",
  "description": [
    "The producer chooses send or idle_p each round; the consumer chooses recv or idle_c, and after a reception it is BUSY for one round with probability p_busy (it then has to rest).",
    "Why a transformation over joint actions? PRISM-games rejects a channel with two independent commands [send] and [recv] when both fire in the same round, and a shared global counter silently loses one of the two updates. The generated module ch1 lists [send,recv] (push + pop), [send,b] (push) and [a,recv] (pop) explicitly.",
    "A player cannot be blocked: when the channel is full, a send is dropped (reward ch1_lost)."
  ],
  "objectives": [
    "Understand why asynchronous communication needs a joint-action channel in a CSG.",
    "Use coalitions to ask who can cause or prevent message loss."
  ],
  "exercises": [
    "Predict first: can the producer alone force a loss (<<prod>>)? Can the consumer alone prevent it (<<cons>>)?",
    "Increase CAP_ch1: how does the probability that the producer forces an overflow within k rounds change?",
    "Open PRISM Code and count the commands of ch1: how does it grow with the number of actions of each player?"
  ],
  "parameters": [
    {
      "name": "k",
      "label": "Number of rounds",
      "value": "10"
    },
    {
      "name": "CAP_ch1",
      "label": "Channel capacity",
      "value": "2"
    },
    {
      "name": "p_busy",
      "label": "P(consumer busy after a reception)",
      "value": "0.5"
    }
  ],
